      // A DISLIKE must be decided BEFORE the wide prohibition patterns below. `싫어`
      // and `좋아하지 않` contain substrings the boundary rule also matches (`않는다`,
      // `하지 않`), so testing boundary first stored the plain dislike
      // "민트차를 좋아하지 않는다" as a BOUNDARY (measured). A dislike is a matter of
      // taste, not a constraint on what may be done, so the narrower, unambiguous
      // dislike forms are read first.
      if (/(싫어(?:해|한다|하다)?|싫다|좋아하지\s*않|더\s*이상\s*좋아하지|좋아하지\s*않는다|dislikes?|hates?|don['’]?t\s+like|doesn['’]?t\s+like|no\s+longer\s+likes?)/i.test(raw)) return 'dislike';
